  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Determine all real eigenvalues $\\lambda $ of the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 6\\end{aligned}].$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we find the matrix $\\,A-\\lambda I:$\n\ufeff\ufeff\ufeff\n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}4-\\lambda  & 0 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 3-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 6-\\lambda \\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation is $det(A-\\lambda I)=0.$ \n\nSince the determinant of a triangular (or diagonal) matrix is given by the product of the entries on the main diagonal, we obtain the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}4-\\lambda  & 0 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 3-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 6-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )(3-\\lambda )(6-\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -4)(\\lambda -3)(\\lambda -6) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}=3,$ $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=4,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=6.$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvalues of a triangular (or diagonal) matrix are always the entries on the main diagonal.",
